}

const FACTS = `
Target: a single-block SHA256 circuit (fixed IV, 512 free message input bits) as an acyclic graph of NAND / constant / output nodes. Optimizer works on an internal And-Inverter Graph (AIG: AND nodes + inverted edges), lowered back to NAND/C/O. In this NAND basis a 2-input XOR costs 4 NAND (irreducible minimum), a full adder's optimum is 9 NAND.

What is ALREADY achieved and MEASURED (so don't re-propose these):
- Base circuit: 392,448 NAND. After strash (structural hashing = constant folding + common-subexpression elimination + dead-cone): 253,620 (-35%). After SAT-confirmed FRAIG (functional node merging, proven via miters): 239,780 NAND (-39%). AIG core ~228,973 AND nodes; lowering adds ~10,800 inverters (~4.5%).
- Measured in ISOLATION via the real optimizer: a full adder optimizes to 9 NAND (textbook optimum); a 32-bit ripple adder to 8.84 NAND/bit (optimal). Ch=(e&f)^(~e&g) optimizes to 4 NAND/bit = optimal 2:1 MUX. Sigma0/Sigma1/sigma0/sigma1 are GF(2)-linear, each a circulant XOR of 3 rotated/shifted copies with DISTINCT rotation offsets -> provably SHARING-FREE within each (64 XOR2 = ~256 NAND, no reducible pairs) -> already ~optimal. Maj=(a&b)^(a&c)^(b&c) currently optimizes to 11 NAND/bit but the OR-form optimizes to 6 -> ~5 NAND/bit slack x 2048 maj bits ~ 5% (a cut-rewrite or Boolean restructure would capture it).
- FRAIG (functional merging) yielded only ~5% because the generator already shares heavily (working-variable copies are by-reference, the a^b term is shared inside each adder, rotations are free wiring).
- Node budget is adder-dominated: ~600 add32 operations x 32 bits ~ 19,200 full adders ~ 172k NAND; Sigma/sigma ~57k; Ch/Maj smaller.

